<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="light">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Squire Reading Stack 4: Short Excerpts and Guided Readings</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@picocss/pico@1/css/pico.min.css" />
    <style>
      body {
        max-width: 880px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 32px 80px;
        position: relative;
        box-sizing: border-box;
        font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times, serif;
        font-size: 12pt;
        line-height: 1.5;
        color: #000000;
        text-align: justify;
        background-color: #ffffff;
      }
      h1 {
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 24pt;
        font-weight: bold;
        margin-bottom: 24px;
        color: #000000;
        font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times, serif;
      }
      h2 {
        font-size: 14pt;
        font-weight: bold;
        margin-top: 24px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
        color: #000000;
        font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times, serif;
      }
      p {
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        text-indent: 0.5in;
      }
      .excerpt-box {
        border: 1px solid #000000;
        padding: 16px 24px;
        margin: 16px 0;
        text-indent: 0;
      }
      .excerpt-box p {
        margin-bottom: 0;
        text-indent: 0;
      }
      .prompt {
        font-style: italic;
        font-weight: bold;
        margin-top: 16px;
        display: block;
      }
      hr {
        border: 0;
        border-top: 1px solid #000000;
        margin: 24px 0;
      }
      .note {
        font-style: italic;
        text-indent: 0;
        margin-bottom: 24px;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Squire Reading Stack 4: Short Excerpts and Guided Readings</h1>

    <p class="note">
      <strong>Instructor's Note:</strong> Welcome to the fourth reading stack in your Squire School training. These
      short, supervised readings are designed to help you understand the history, virtues, and purpose of the Order.
      Please read each excerpt carefully and reflect on the questions provided. These readings support your preparation
      for advancement.
    </p>

    <h2>Week 1: Origins and Virtues</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        After the First Crusade, the roads leading to Jerusalem remained very dangerous. Christian pilgrims traveling to
        the Holy Land faced many threats on their journey. The Order was originally formed with a simple, charitable
        mission: to protect these vulnerable travelers. This mission required the virtues of honesty, courage,
        obedience, charity, and reverence.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: Why is protecting those who are weak or traveling considered a form of both charity and
        courage?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 2: Founding People and Places</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        When studying the founders of the Order, such as Hugh de Payns, it is easy to repeat exciting stories or myths.
        However, as a Squire, you are taught to value truth before legend. Real history must be based on careful
        evidence and reliable sources, rather than repeating stories without proof. Honesty is the most important virtue
        when separating historical fact from legend.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: Why is honesty important when learning about the history of an organization?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 3: Approval and Mission</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        The early Order was not just an ordinary army; it was recognized by the Council of Troyes as a religious order.
        This means that its members lived a disciplined life focused on worship and prayer. Their purpose was bound to
        spiritual duties and service to others, which made their mission entirely different from seeking military glory
        or conquest.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: How does a focus on prayer and worship change the way an organization carries out its
        duties?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 4: Structure and Daily Life</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        To serve effectively, an organization needs structure and discipline. The Order relied on different classes,
        such as Knights, Sergeants, and Chaplains, as well as leaders like the Grand Master and Seneschal. But no matter
        the title a member held, or the clothing and symbols they wore, the core rule was always "service before self."
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: Why is dependable service more important than wearing a special symbol or holding a leadership
        title?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 5: Life of the Order</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        The daily life of the Order was rarely about grand events. It involved routine duties, guarding castles and
        roads, practicing daily prayer, and giving charity to the poor. A personal rule of conduct helps maintain this
        quiet, steady faithfulness. Routine life often reveals true character far better than dramatic moments.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: Why does steady, everyday discipline often show more character than a single moment of
        bravery?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 6: Power and Crisis</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        As the Order grew powerful and wealthy, it faced new challenges. Success can sometimes cause an organization to
        forget its original purpose, a problem known as "mission drift." The fall of the city of Acre in 1291 was a
        major historical crisis that severely weakened the Order's original protective mission, forcing them to navigate
        a dangerous new reality.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: How can wealth, power, and success make it harder to remember a simple mission of
        service?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 7: Suppression and Courage</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        In the early 1300s, the Order faced false accusations, arrests ordered by Philip IV, and eventual suppression.
        During these dark times, members had to show great endurance. True courage means facing pressure, fear, and
        injustice with dignity and honesty, rather than responding with panic or anger.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: What does true courage look like when a person is facing false accusations or very difficult
        times?</span
      >
    </div>

    <h2>Week 8: Review and Board Preparation</h2>
    <div class="excerpt-box">
      <p>
        Your time as a Squire prepares you for future advancement to the rank of Sergeant. Readiness is not just about
        memorizing names and dates on a historical timeline. It is about showing good character, dependably completing
        your service hours, understanding truthful history, and acting with composure and respect during your oral
        review board.
      </p>
      <span class="prompt"
        >Think About It: How do your reading habits, memory work, and daily conduct prove that you are ready for the
        next step?</span
      >
    </div>

    <hr />

    <p class="note">
      <strong>Final Reminder:</strong> Please remember to record these readings in your official reading log. Practice
      your memory work diligently, and bring your thoughts on these prompts to our moderated discussions. Remember that
      a true Squire approaches learning with honesty, respect, and a willingness to serve.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
